<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as integrateBlock from './statements/integrate-block.md';
</script>

An [improper Gaussian factor](../improper-gaussian/) was introduced by an integral: what is left of
a model of normals on a quantity's neighbours once the quantity is integrated out. The examples
there were integrals done by hand. [Elimination](../square-root-elimination/) does the same
integral by an orthogonal factorization of the rows, and this note states what it leaves, in
general: a factor of the same kind on the blocks that were tied to the one eliminated, which need
not determine them.

## The integral

<Statement {...integrateBlock} />

<Proof>

The first part is part 2 of [%full](statement:math/square-root-elimination/eliminate-one), with
the constant $c$ carried along: $e^{\logterm} = e^{c - \phi}$. The right-hand side is a squared
affine residual in $\tangent_S$, a linear term and a constant, which is the definition
[%full](statement:math/improper-gaussian/factor), and nothing in it requires $A'$ to have full
rank. For the second, take $n \ne 0$ with $A_k n = 0$; along the line $\tangent_k + t n$ the squares
do not change and $\logterm$ changes linearly, by $-t\, \eta_k\transpose n$, so $e^{\logterm}$ is
constant or grows exponentially along the line and its integral over $\R^{d_k}$ diverges.

</Proof>

That is an improper Gaussian factor of the variables left: its rows are the rows the
factorization did not use, its linear term is what is left of the linear terms, and its constant
collects the rest. Nothing requires $A'$ to have full rank, and when the eliminated block was the
only thing tying down some direction of $\tangent_S$, it has not. The factor is the exact answer
all the same.

The integral is over the block's tangent coordinates, against Lebesgue measure. On a Euclidean
space that is the integral over the quantity itself. On a curved or a positive space it is the
integral of the factor as written in the chart at the anchor, and an integral against the space's
own measure carries the chart's volume, as
[](eq:math/map-estimation/chart-objective) does for a mode.

**What the factor is.** Repeated block by block, the statement integrates any set of blocks out of
a sum of factors, in any order, leaving a sum of factors on the rest; when the rows left determine
every direction, the sum is a normal, and its mean and precision are those of the marginal of
[%full](statement:math/square-root-elimination/marginal). Restricted to the factors joined to the
blocks integrated out, as in [](eq:math/square-root-elimination/replace), it is the single factor
on their [Markov blanket](../generative-models/#graphical-representations) that replaces them:
the factor that a model keeps when it forgets a quantity.

**A block that is not determined.** The second part says when a block cannot be integrated out:
when its own rows do not determine it, the integral over it is infinite. A landmark seen from one
pose along one direction, with no prior, cannot be integrated out, since its rows say nothing of it
across that direction; the pose, which has a prior, can be, and leaves on the landmark a factor that
is flat along the bearing, the first example of the
[improper factor's note](../improper-gaussian/#two-examples). The elimination's diagonal reports
which: a block whose triangular root has a zero diagonal entry has no integral.

**The order of integration.** Integrating out is elimination by part 2 of
[%full](statement:math/square-root-elimination/eliminate-one), and elimination by part 1 leaves
the same rows and differs only in the constant. So the order that keeps the factorization cheap,
the [elimination tree](../square-root-elimination/#order-fill-and-the-elimination-tree), is the
order that keeps integrating out cheap, and a chain of quantities integrated out from one end is
eliminated with no fill at all. The [next note](../kalman-filter/) is that chain.
